        <- [The circumstances were quite exceptional]

<No reason was shown>: Nothing in the evidence explains why a new session needed five weeks rather than the few days such a thing normally takes.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "[58]-[61]",
     note: "Convergent. Each source of evidence independently fails to supply a reason, and the conclusion is drawn from their joint silence. The form has no counterpart in the philosophical list: it is an inference from the absence of a justification on the evidence before the court, and the hedge 'on the evidence which has been put before us' is load-bearing."}

<No reason was shown>

(1) [The Government must be accorded a great deal of latitude]: Of course, the Government must be accorded a great deal of latitude in making decisions of this nature. #conceded
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "[58]"}
(2) [No reason was given for closing down Parliament for five weeks]: It will be apparent from the documents quoted earlier that no reason was given for closing down Parliament for five weeks.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "[58]"}
(3) [Assembling a legislative programme has never needed five weeks]: Sir John Major has never known a Government to need as much as five weeks to put together its legislative agenda, a typical time being four to six days.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "[59]",
     source: "\"he has never known a Government to need as much as five weeks to put together its legislative agenda\"",
     note: "Unchallenged evidence. Its force is evidential rather than authoritative, which makes it the one premise in this file that works the way a premise in a paper works."}
(4) [The memorandum says nothing about why so long was needed]: The memorandum has much to say about a new session and Queen's Speech but nothing about why so long was needed to prepare for it.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "[60]"}
(5) [The memorandum does not weigh recess against prorogation]: Perhaps most tellingly of all, the memorandum does not address the competing merits of going into recess and prorogation. It wrongly gives the impression that they are much the same.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "[60]"}
(6) [The Prime Minister has a constitutional responsibility to have regard to all relevant interests]
