# Remedy

<Article 9 bars the court>: The court cannot declare the prorogation null, because article 9 of the Bill of Rights and the wider privileges of Parliament put the prorogation beyond question. #reported
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "[63]",
     note: "The Government's argument. The court sets it out in order to reject it."}

<Article 9 bars the court>

(1) [The prorogation was a proceeding in Parliament]: The prorogation itself was a proceeding in Parliament. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "[63]",
     source: "\"a proceeding in Parliament\" which cannot be impugned or questioned in any court"}
    <- [Prorogation cannot sensibly be described as a proceeding in Parliament]
(2) [Proceedings in Parliament may not be questioned in any court]: That the Freedome of Speech and Debates or Proceedings in Parlyament ought not to be impeached or questioned in any Court or Place out of Parlyament. #authority
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "[64]", note: "Article 9 of the Bill of Rights 1688, in the spelling the judgment prints. #authority: it binds the court whatever anyone thinks of it, which an ordinary premise does not."}
-----
(3) [The court cannot declare the prorogation null]: The court cannot declare the prorogation null and of no effect, and reasoning back from that, neither can it question the Order in Council which led to it. #reported
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "[63]",
     source: "\"And reasoning back from that, neither can the Order in Council which led to it.\""}
    -> [The court may consider the validity of the prorogation]

<Prorogation is not a proceeding in Parliament>: Prorogation fails every criterion by which a proceeding in Parliament is identified, so article 9 does not reach it.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "[65]-[68]",
     note: "Application of a criterion. The criteria are taken from R v Chaytor and from Erskine May and then run against the facts of a prorogation."}

<Prorogation is not a proceeding in Parliament>

(1) [It is for the court to determine the scope of Parliamentary privilege]: it is for the court and not for Parliament to determine the scope of Parliamentary privilege
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "[66]", note: "Point (1) of what the court takes R v Chaytor to establish."}
(2) [Article 9 is directed at the core business of Parliament]: The principal matter to which article 9 is directed is freedom of speech and debate in the Houses of Parliament and in parliamentary committees, which is where the core or essential business of Parliament takes place.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "[66]",
     source: "\"freedom of speech and debate in the Houses of Parliament and in parliamentary committees. This is where the core or essential business of Parliament takes place\""}
(3) [A proceeding is a formal action taken by the House in its collective capacity]: The primary meaning of proceedings, as a technical parliamentary term, is some formal action, usually a decision, taken by the House in its collective capacity.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "[67]",
     source: "\"The primary meaning of proceedings, as a technical parliamentary term, which it had at least as early as the 17th century, is some formal action, usually a decision, taken by the House in its collective capacity.\"",
     note: "Erskine May, Parliamentary Practice, quoted by the court."}
(4) [Prorogation is not a decision of either House]: It is not a decision of either House of Parliament. Quite the contrary: it is something which is imposed upon them from outside.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "[68]"}
(5) [Members can neither speak nor vote on a prorogation]: It is not something upon which the Members of Parliament can speak or vote.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "[68]"}
(6) [The Commissioners do not act as members of the House of Lords]: The Commissioners are not acting in their capacity as members of the House of Lords but in their capacity as Royal Commissioners carrying out the Queen's bidding. They have no freedom of speech.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "[68]"}
(7) [Prorogation brings the core business of Parliament to an end]: This is not the core or essential business of Parliament. Quite the contrary: it brings that core or essential business of Parliament to an end.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "[68]"}
-----
(8) [Prorogation cannot sensibly be described as a proceeding in Parliament]: Prorogation cannot sensibly be described as a "proceeding in Parliament".
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "[68]"}
    +> [The court may consider the validity of the prorogation]

<The prorogation was void>: Unlawful advice is a nullity, so everything built on it -- the Order in Council, and the prorogation itself -- is a nullity too.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "[69]",
     note: "Serial, and the court works forward from the advice, which is the reverse of the order in which it reached the question."}

<The prorogation was void>

(1) [The court may consider the validity of the prorogation]: This court is not, therefore, precluded by article 9 or by any wider Parliamentary privilege from considering the validity of the prorogation itself.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "[69]"}
(2) [The advice was unlawful]
(3) [Advice outside the powers of the Prime Minister is null and of no effect]: It was outside the powers of the Prime Minister to give it. This means that it was null and of no effect.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "[69]"}
-- {uses: [1, 2, 3]} --
(4) [The Order in Council was null and of no effect]: The Order in Council, being founded on unlawful advice, was likewise unlawful, null and of no effect and should be quashed.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "[69]",
     source: "\"It led to the Order in Council which, being founded on unlawful advice, was likewise unlawful, null and of no effect and should be quashed.\""}
